X11: create GL contexts through EGL, with GLX as the fallback.

A GL context GDK creates is what a program draws into a GdkWindow with and,
through gdk_cairo_draw_from_gl(), what it composites GL textures of its own
into.  A program whose picture is produced in another process -- a web
engine's user-interface process, whose rendering process draws the page --
receives each frame as a dma-buf descriptor, and the only way to show such a
frame without copying it is to import it as an EGLImage and bind that to a
texture in the toolkit's own context.  That needs the toolkit's context to be
an EGL context on the EGLDisplay the image was created on.  A GLX context
cannot take an EGLImage at all, so under GLX the program maps the buffer and
copies it through the processor once per frame, at the full size of the
window: on this system about 7ms of every maximized frame, which is the
difference between 60 and 30 frames a second.

The Wayland and Windows backends already create their contexts through EGL.
This is the same arrangement for the X11 backend, modelled on
gdkglcontext-wayland.c.  What is X11-specific: the display is an EGL display
on the Xlib connection; an X window is itself an EGL native window, so there
is nothing to create or resize between the two; and config selection has to
name a config whose native visual is the window's, because EGL puts a surface
on a window only from such a config and eglChooseConfig() ignores that
attribute when selecting, so configs are enumerated and matched by hand.

EGL is tried first when a screen brings GL up, and only where it can serve a
window on the screen's default visual; otherwise the GLX path runs exactly as
before.  GDK_GL=glx forces GLX for a session.  A display is one or the other
for its whole life.  The GL-capable default visuals are cached on the root
window under GDK_VISUALS_EGL, beside the GLX path's GDK_VISUALS, so a session
that switches backends does not read the other's choice as its own.

gdk_x11_display_get_glx_version() answers FALSE on a display driven through
EGL -- it is asked about GLX -- and GLX_EXT_texture_from_pixmap is not
offered there, so gdkgl.c's generic texture upload takes over for cairo
surfaces.

